18. Current Frontend Problem and Correct Architecture
The dashboard has gone through multiple pasted versions. One version contains an internal apiRequest() helper, while the intended architecture separates API handling into api.js. This duplication is a major source of frontend inconsistency.

File
Single responsibility
api.js
Central HTTP client: token, Authorization header, JSON parsing, 401 handling, errors.
auth.js
Login/register/OTP/password-reset UI and auth-related redirects.
dashboard.js
Dashboard state, navigation, webhook CRUD UI, delivery UI.
dashboard.html
Structure and DOM elements only.
style.css
Visual design only.
The clean architecture is: dashboard.js calls API.get/post/put/delete; api.js is the only place that knows how the JWT is attached to requests.

19. Exact Create Webhook End-to-End Flow
1. User logs in.
2. Backend returns JWT.
3. Frontend stores JWT as access_token.
4. Dashboard loads.
5. dashboard.js calls GET /auth/me.
6. Backend decodes JWT.
7. Backend loads User by payload["sub"].
8. Dashboard receives user information.
9. dashboard.js calls GET /webhooks/.
10. Backend filters Webhook.user_id by current_user.id.
11. User clicks + Create webhook.
12. Modal opens.
13. User enters name and target URL.
14. dashboard.js sends:
       POST /webhooks/
       Authorization: Bearer <JWT>
       JSON body
15. FastAPI validates WebhookCreate schema.
16. get_current_user identifies user.
17. SQLAlchemy creates Webhook.
18. secrets.token_urlsafe() generates secret.
19. db.commit().
20. db.refresh().
21. Backend returns webhook.
22. Frontend closes modal.
23. Frontend reloads webhook list.
24. PostgreSQL now contains the webhook.
25. Dashboard displays it.

This is the first end-to-end workflow to make completely reliable before adding more UI features.

20. Exact Delivery End-to-End Flow
1. An event enters the events layer.
2. The backend identifies the target webhook.
3. Ownership/authorization is checked.
4. A delivery record is created.
5. webhook_service.deliver_webhook() sends the request.
6. Signature is generated from the webhook secret.
7. Target application receives the request.
8. Target returns an HTTP status.
9. Delivery status is saved:
      2xx → success
      non-2xx → failed
      exception → failed + error_message
10. Dashboard calls GET /deliveries/.
11. Delivery history is rendered.
12. User can inspect/retry a delivery.
13. Retry increments attempt_number and records the new result.

