23. Error Handling Map
Status
Typical meaning in HookRelay
400
Bad input / invalid request depending on route.
401
Missing/invalid/expired authentication or invalid signature.
403
Authenticated but not allowed, e.g. email not verified.
404
User/webhook/delivery does not exist or is not owned.
409
Conflict such as duplicate email.
422
FastAPI/Pydantic validation error.
500
Unexpected server-side exception; inspect traceback.
24. Alembic and Schema Evolution
Change SQLAlchemy model
        ↓
alembic revision --autogenerate -m "description"
        ↓
Inspect migration file
        ↓
alembic upgrade head
        ↓
PostgreSQL schema updated

The password-reset fields were added through an Alembic revision in the project. This establishes the expected migration workflow.

Do not rely on Base.metadata.create_all() to update an existing production schema. create_all creates missing tables/objects but does not perform controlled schema migrations for existing structures.

25. Environment Variables and Secrets
Secret/config
Where it belongs
DATABASE_URL
.env / deployment environment
JWT_SECRET_KEY
.env / deployment environment
Email provider credentials
.env / deployment environment
Other API credentials
.env / deployment environment
Never commit .env to Git. Never paste actual secret values into frontend JavaScript. Never expose webhook secrets unnecessarily.

26. Security Requirements Before Production
Use a strong JWT secret of at least 32 random bytes for HS256.

The current project has produced an InsecureKeyLengthWarning for a short JWT key; this must be fixed before production.

Do not hardcode webhook secrets in test_receiver.

Do not return secrets repeatedly after they are created unless intentionally designed.

Rotate webhook secrets safely and document the impact on consumers.

Use strict ownership filters on every webhook and delivery query.

Validate target URLs and define SSRF protections before allowing arbitrary internet destinations in a production webhook service.

Add request timeouts to outbound webhook HTTP calls.

Consider retry/backoff and idempotency for production delivery.

Use restrictive CORS in production instead of allow_origins=['*'].

Store only password hashes, never plaintext passwords.

Expire and invalidate password-reset tokens after use.

Avoid logging JWTs, reset tokens, passwords, or webhook secrets.

